WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:01.920
This video is sponsored by dashlane

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.399
Climate change is scary.

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:11.339
It's possibly the biggest threat civilization has ever faced to very briefly summarize human activity has increased carbon dioxide concentrations in the atmosphere

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.060
Increases in CO2 must have a warming effect on the surface because, physics.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.960
the earth is warmed by approximately one degree on average since the eighteenth century

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.740
and our best models of the Earth's system can really reproduce this warming if human influence on the climate is included

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:31.280
So humans have definitely changed the global climate for the warmer by emitting CO2 and other gases like methane and we're

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:37.919
continuing to emit those gases every year the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change warns that if we don't limit further emissions,

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.920
then we will see further warming but also

00:00:40.149 --> 00:00:45.959
Rising sea levels more extreme hot weather events and increases in the frequency of both droughts and heavy precipitation

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.760
This will result in society-wide

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.620
changes and most probably

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.189
extensive conflict over migration and resources

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:57.120
If you're anything like me you're used to hearing about climate change in this tone

00:00:57.399 --> 00:01:01.649
Described in much the same way as winter in Game of Thrones winter is coming. It's coming

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.120
There's nothing we can do to stop it

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.580
It's definitely coming and when it gets here, it's going to be really put -owp nevermind too late

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.110
Solutions to climate change are rarely discussed because well

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:16.060
It's a very complex problem everyone on earth contributes to the greenhouse gas emissions which fuel climate change though not equally.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:20.580
These contributions take place on an individual level through our daily activities

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:26.129
But take place through networks of actors: the companies we choose to do business with,

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:31.019
the banks we trust with our money, the governments that we choose to elect. Our choices, in this regard, in the

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.500
larger scale of the economy and political landscape have truly enormous repercussions.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:39.600
When people write articles on what we can do to mitigate climate change

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.869
they very much focus on the individual actions we take, for example:

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:46.589
not eating meat or using public transport and these are things we should be doing

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:53.008
I'm not arguing against individual action to combat climate change, but consider that we are endpoint users in a vast network

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:57.629
which we have ultimate collective control over; and a far more effective way of reducing

00:01:57.629 --> 00:02:03.640
our greenhouse gas emissions and preventing climate catastrophe is to change the workings of that network from the top-down

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:04.920
Over the course of this video,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:10.940
I'm going to make three recommendations of what we, as voters and activists, can do to accomplish just that.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:16.020
To begin with consider a breakdown of where greenhouse gas emissions come from by sector.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.520
While agriculture, industry, and forestry all make modest contributions,

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.360
the overwhelming majority of humanity's greenhouse gas emissions come from energy use

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:29.520
Note that that's different from electricity generation. Energy use encompasses lots of things including

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.290
Electricity generation, but also heating, cooling and cooking, transportation,

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:35.900
manufacturing, and other miscellaneous uses of energy.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:40.800
If we're to beat climate change then, we need to revolutionize how we use energy in a general sense:

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:47.460
transitioning away from fossil fuels like coal and gas to renewable sources such as wind solar geothermal

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.140
and hydropower. The role of nuclear power is a...

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:56.519
...topic for a future video. Accomplishing a society-wide energy transformation is a daunting task

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:02.639
but the world's energy experts have been thinking about this for a long time. The International nonprofit, REN21,

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:09.420
Based at the UN Environment Programme has produced a document every year since 2005 called the global status report

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.059
The GSR is written with contributions from energy experts politicians and researchers from around the world

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:18.660
Assessing where we're at with the renewable energy transition

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.859
The full report is available for anyone to read link in the description. Having read it,

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.850
I want to make three arguments for what we should be focusing on to

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.360
Accelerate the energy transition bring greenhouse gas emissions down and beat climate change.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:37.860
Firstly, targets need to be set by politicians for renewables uptake beyond electricity generation

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:44.580
We're all familiar with the drive to replace coal-fired power stations with wind turbines, nuclear, and solar power plants

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:51.480
Of the 197 countries in the world, 146 have targets for the use of renewable technology to generate electricity

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.920
and these targets are working

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:59.120
In 2017, 26.5% of energy used as electricity came from renewable sources

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:04.440
However, as we saw earlier energy use means much more than just electrical power. In particular,

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:09.869
It means transport and heating and the uptake of modern renewables in these sectors is much lower:

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:16.960
10.3% for heating and just 0.3% for transport. The likely reason? A lack of targets.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:22.940
Only 48 countries have renewable energy targets for heating and cooking and only 42 for transport.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.580
In order for the energy transition to happen across all sectors,

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:31.700
Governments must commit to targets for renewable energy use in heating and in transportation

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.920
So what can you do? Ask your local representative about this

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.880
and vote for a political party that pledges to implement these targets

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:41.160
Secondly, and I'm going to say this appropriately loudly,

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:42.560
(wait for it)

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.100
PHASE OUT FOSSIL FUEL SUBSIDIES

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.400
There is a common perception that renewable energy is fine and dandy in principle,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.060
But just too expensive to make economic sense in reality.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.140
This is completely wrong.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:00.060
In this terribly formatted figure from the International Renewable Energy Agency,

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:06.040
the cost per kilowatt hour of fossil fuel power generation is compared to that of various renewable power sources.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.860
In 2010 and 2017, almost all of the technologies shown are either cost competitive or

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.580
substantially cheaper per kilowatt hour than traditional fossil fuels and getting cheaper.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.720
People assume that renewables are only used because of subsidies

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:22.440
and incentives given to renewable power suppliers

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.420
and this can't be further from the truth.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:28.140
Fossil fuels are subsidized significantly more than renewables.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:33.860
In 2016, 370 billion dollars were given in subsidies for fossil fuel use and only

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:38.500
140 billion given to renewables. If you factor in other externalities like

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:44.100
health and pollution, then subsidies given to fossil fuels are an order of magnitude greater

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:49.499
So despite renewables being increasingly the lowest cost option (In fact in some cases last year,

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.149
it was cheaper to build new renewables generation than to maintain old fossil fuel capacity)

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:59.040
money is still given to subsidize and develop fossil fuel use by governments

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:03.380
The G20 has committed to phasing out "inefficient fossil fuel subsidies"

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:09.940
but hasn't defined "inefficient" or done anything on the subject since saying so in... 2009

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:14.680
It is imperative that our Governments commits to rapidly phasing out fossil fuel subsidies

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.500
stopping distortion of the power market and allowing the energy transition to take place

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:21.760
Again, write to your local representative about this

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:25.140
and vote for a political party that pledges to phase out these subsidies.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:30.700
Lastly, more money needs to be invested in renewable energy, in particular, in developing countries

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.780
It's estimated by the International Energy Agency that to keep global temperature rise below 2 degrees Celsius,

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:42.940
12 trillion dollars must be invested in renewable power supplier between now and 2014.

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:46.700
That translates to about five hundred billion dollars a year, or roughly

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.760
twice what the world is currently investing.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:53.120
At the moment, we're not going to hit the targets set by the Paris agreements.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:54.940
We need to be more ambitious

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:59.000
Interestingly, while most investment in renewable energy in 2017 came from

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:02.960
developed countries, the top three being: China, the USA, and Japan,

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:06.140
the highest spend per capita was in developing countries.

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:10.920
It's estimated that 90% of people currently with our electricity access will gain this access through renewables

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:15.840
mostly off-grid distributed renewables in developing countries. In this process will take approximately

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:21.980
50 billion dollars a year in private and public funds, and is essential to fund

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:27.060
to continue development of these countries without committing to massive further emissions. As such,

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:32.060
Projects in developing countries using renewable energy generation must be prioritized

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.340
by development banks and by foreign aid

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:39.960
And absolutely prioritized over projects using fossil fuels. Further investment in developed countries,

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:47.520
which fell more than 18% in 2017 must continue. In particular, in the flexibility of energy infrastructure.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.540
Man, I really hope there's at least one Energy Minister that ends up watching this.

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:51.840
Otherwise, that line was pointless

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:57.220
So if you live in a developing country, vote for a political party which prioritizes

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:00.840
investment in economically viable renewable energy over fossil fuels.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:04.780
Well, if you're lucky enough to have a say in where charity or development money goes,

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:09.100
Prioritize investment in renewable energy access in the developing world.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.780
There are myriad aspects to the energy transition.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:16.780
I didn't even touch on the issue of cooking, or biofuels, or carbon taxes for example.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.580
This video could never hold all the answers to our climate problems

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:23.160
Hell if it did, I will be holding a Nobel Peace Prize in my hands right now

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:27.420
What I hope it's demonstrated is that there are specific meaningful actions you can take

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:30.760
beyond the individual level to combat climate change

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:32.440
Make your voice heard with your vote,

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:35.060
Write to your local representative about these issues

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:39.740
Share this video so others can learn from it too, and spread the word about these three points

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:42.869
Which we should all be demanding from our governments

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:49.379
Anthropogenic climate change is an issue that has taken a world to create and we'll take a world to unmake

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:53.680
We've already damaged the planet in ways that will take thousands of years to repair

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.820
but, through collective action, we can turn the tide

00:08:56.860 --> 00:09:02.300
Limit our greenhouse gas emissions and limit the further damage we do to the world of our children.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.820
The best time for action was 20 years ago

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:07.589
the second best time... is now

